Packaging
---------

The port is done: all three platforms build, are real CI gates, and have been
run and heard on real hardware. One thing it never covered.

- The macOS .app has never been run on a Mac without GTK on it -- which is
  every ordinary Mac, and is what bundling GTK into it is for. CI cannot check
  this: building at all means Homebrew put GTK on the runner, so a bundle that
  resolved against it looks like one carrying its own. macOS does run, but that
  was built from source against the GTK it needed to build in the first place,
  which is a different claim.

  Windows is done -- the zip has been unpacked and run on a machine that has
  never had GTK. Homebrew's keg symlinks and the two-prefix problem are macOS
  only, so that does not carry over.

  Does not apply to Linux, where the package is meant to use the
  distribution's GTK.  (docs/PACKAGING.md)


Never heard
-----------

- No MIDI has ever been played through the RtMidi path, on any platform. The
  headless checks pass; that is not the same thing.


The corpus
----------

- Five plugins lost their last user in the corpus when dsp/old and its
  neighbors were cleared out: dist::clip, filt::res1pole, math::sin,
  osc::bandosc and osc::multisined. Nothing loads or renders them now, so
  nothing notices if they break. Either give each a graph or retire it.

- Nothing ships the whole piano. grand.dsp is the strings and the hammer;
  the pedal's resonance is fx/sympathetic.dsp, a channel effect, and the
  room is fx/hall.dsp. No patch or piece puts them together, so the
  sympathetic work is only heard by someone who assembles the chain by
  hand. A Grand patch, and a short piece to show it off.

- grand.dsp's note-on cost has not been measured in the browser again since
  it was cut. Through the wasm module on a desktop, one note-on cost about
  1.4 ms of a quantum -- half the 2.67 ms a 128-frame quantum has at 48 kHz
  -- and a six-note chord about 7 ms; a phone is several times slower, so
  the grand clicked on every note there, and the web page opens patch mode
  on ladder.dsp rather than on it. Natively, since: the hammer solver starts
  from the last sample's forces (a 16-note chord's first window 175M -> 66M
  instructions), a note restarts a finished voice instead of copying the
  tree (addNote about 8x fewer instructions), and constant expressions cost
  one value a window. Re-measure with a variant of wasm/web/bench.mjs that
  holds a chord on one .dsp, on the desktop and on a phone, and move the
  default patch if it is now cheap enough. scripts/dspbench is the native
  side of the same measurement.

- grand.dsp's damper falls in one stage. filt::pianostring's damper is one
  per-trip loss on every string and every partial, so a released note falls
  in a straight line in dB. A real damped note drops fast and then leaves a
  quiet tail of a few hundred ms -- the out-of-phase unison motion the felt
  barely touches -- with the upper partials gone before the fundamental.
  Either spare part of the out-of-phase motion from the damper's loss or
  make that loss rise with frequency. There is no damper-felt noise either.
  The damper times themselves are guesses: the Iowa recordings the rest was
  fitted to hold every note to its end, so fitting them wants a recording
  with key releases in it.


Coverage
--------

- dspcheck should grow a "load this and process it" check for anything the
  editor writes.

- A dspstress TSan job is deliberately out of the default matrix: TSan cannot
  be combined with ASan so it needs its own build tree, and the run is slow.
  Worth a nightly schedule: trigger once the rest is stable.

- thSynthTree::buildSynthTree walks the graph recursively with a recalc flag
  rather than a proper topological order. An editor makes it easy to build
  pathological graphs.


Engine
------

- RT-safety, what is left of it. The per-window path is clear: thMidiChan no
  longer builds an std::string per channel per note per window, no longer
  searches a std::map for it, and no longer lets thSynthTree::getArg invent
  and insert the arg it could not find; the two VLAs are buffers owned by the
  channel. Audio is bit-identical across the corpus. Still outstanding, and
  none of it per window:

    * A freshly copied note's args are resized from 1 to windowlen on its
      first window, so every plugin holding state allocates once per note,
      on the audio thread. A voice restarted from the channel's pool does
      not -- thArg keeps its capacity and zero-fills in place -- so this is
      now the first `poly' notes of a channel, and any note struck while
      every pooled voice is sounding. Sizing the args when the note is built
      would move the rest to the GUI thread.
    * insertNote and releaseNote reach `trigger' by name. Per key press.
    * buildSynthTree's recursive recalc walk, above.  (docs/ARCHITECTURE.md)

- Performance left on the table, measured on grand.dsp and not yet done:

    * filt::pianostring is most of what a grand voice costs once it is
      sounding. Keeping its state in double rather than rounding it to
      float every sample would drop the conversions; it changes the output
      slightly, as the hammer's warm start did. SIMD, in the browser build
      and natively, was tried and measured no faster.
    * The grand holds 48 voices, each until it falls 80 dB, and at 15 to
      40 s a note that is a lot of voices under a sustained passage. Ending
      a voice sooner once it is inaudible under the others is the biggest
      lever left, and a question about the sound rather than the code.

- philly and mirrorball are still over budget on a slow machine: 154 and
  54 over-budget quanta a minute in Chromium on a 4-core cloud VM, against
  4 and 3 on the desktop #390 measured. The quanta that run over mostly
  start no note; it is the steady load, a p50 of 0.74 ms of the 2.67 ms a
  quantum has (philly). Natively, delay::chorus and osc::simple are half
  of it: tonewheel.dsp's ten sines and scanner and section.dsp's chorus,
  per voice, then rhodes.dsp. Tabling osc::multiwave's sines measured no
  faster. What is left changes the sound: one scanner per organ, as on a
  Hammond, rather than one per voice; the section's chorus on the
  channel; fewer tonewheels where a drawbar is down.

- Most args have no description. The ones that do are every one an author
  had already written a comment about, and the rest are deliberately empty
  rather than invented -- but somebody who knows these DSPs could write
  them, and a tooltip is the cheapest documentation this tree has. Likewise
  the declared defaults are only the ones the plugins special-case zero
  for; others may have a sensible default nobody has written down.
  (docs/ARCHITECTURE.md)

- A @chanarg cannot be driven by a node. "This parameter varies with an LFO"
  works today; "the knob moves on its own" needs engine work.


Composition
-----------

- Fitness from the sound itself, for `gen::evolve'. `gen::breed' listens now:
  `target' names an instrument or a sound file, the host renders each genome
  through the chain's instrument on a private synth (src/thcAudition.cpp) and
  answers with the distance libthink/thSoundFeat.h measures, through
  `thcAudition' in thcomposer.h. `evolve' breeds phrases, and a phrase's
  sound is a rendered bar rather than a note; the ear would need a
  `hear' that takes a note list, and the same asynchronous flow breed has.
  Live, a piece that listens replays exactly only offline (genwav): the
  wall clock decides which cycle a generation lands on.

- A composer that changes the *shape* of an instrument's graph, not just a
  number in it. `gen::swap' replaces a channel's whole graph by name and
  `gen::reshape' moves one constant inside the graph that is there; what
  neither can do is add a node, remove one, or rewire what feeds what.
  `THC_EV_NODEARG' refuses an arg that is wired to anything -- another node's
  output, a @chanarg, a note property -- precisely because writing a number
  over a wire is an add/remove/rewire edit wearing a value edit's clothes,
  and that is the refusal this would have to replace with a mechanism.

  What it wants is a third structure edit beside `THC_EV_PATCH' and
  `THC_EV_NODEARG', delivered the way those are: built on the GUI thread,
  swapped through the command queue, scheduled and sparse and drawn on the
  roll, so a composer still cannot thrash a graph faster than its event
  stream flows. The legal moves are already written down --
  `NodeGraph::canConnect' is the constraint function, so a mutated graph can
  be well-formed by construction rather than by luck -- and `loadTree' has
  been a live atomic instrument swap since before any of this existed.
  `thinklang' is pure, so candidate graphs parse off to one side without
  touching the live synth, and `NodeEdit' is the one-writer splice seam a
  program edits text through.

  Past that is the graph as a genome. scripts/dspmatch does it offline:
  `grow' splices a node into a wire, puts an envelope or LFO on a constant,
  or adds a voice, each edit raced with its own tuning against the graph as
  it stands, and the genome is the .dsp text through NodeEdit::Text. What it
  cannot do is edit an expression, which is where most shipped patches sum
  their oscillators, and it is a tool rather than a composer: the third
  structure edit above is what would let a piece do it live.

- Meta chains: a chain that schedules or chooses among the sections rather
  than emitting notes, so the form of a piece is composed the way its notes
  are. `section' landed and is the data half of this; what is missing is
  something that reads it. The other road -- meta-composers splicing the
  piece file itself through `thcGenEdit', so a piece rewrites itself and Save
  publishes what it became -- verifies just as well and reads a great deal
  stranger. Data first.

- Writing a graph out *inline* in a `.gen', rather than naming a `.dsp' by
  file. An `instrument' block carries a filename and the chanarg values that
  make it this instrument, which delivered the self-contained piece; putting
  the graph itself in the same file needs the two grammars merged, and the
  merge is still waiting on a payoff rather than blocking one.
  (docs/UNIFICATION.md)


Controllers
-----------

- A binding in the either-hand block of the player's hid/index replaces the
  controller's own bindings of that control, and so also undoes the
  controller's own per-hand `ignore` of it: the default right hand ignores
  grip_force so only the left hand's squeeze drives level, but a player's
  `grip_force = "level";` for either hand has both hands of a pair drive it,
  the idle one pulling it back whenever its grip block changes. Whether a
  per-hand ignore should outlast an either-hand binding, or the pane should
  warn, is undecided. (docs/HID.md, "Your own profile")


The browser page
----------------

- A clicked Life, CA or evolve board is still not written back on the
  page: their pictures are states the piece goes on changing, so capture
  is a deliberate act, and the page has no button for it yet (the desktop's
  is in the Selection pane). Grids, the euclid ring and accent's steps are
  written at each gesture's end (THC_INPUT_EDITS).  (wasm/web/thinkweb.cpp)

- Two for mullion rather than this tree. `panes.commands()': what can be
  split, what is zoomed and what is in the drawer, as data -- `{ id,
  label, run, enabled }' -- so a tab's right-click, a View menu and a
  command palette can list the tiler's commands, which today are Alt chords
  and nothing else. About forty lines, and the seam a menu bar would sit on
  later, as a module of its own. And popover.js on the `popover' attribute
  and CSS anchor positioning, keeping the JS clamp as the fallback: a
  popover in the top layer is clipped by nothing, which is the whole reason
  overlay() exists. Worth checking before more is built on overlay().

- Four measurements the browser build has only ever been given on a fast
  desktop. The scheduler runs in the worklet on numbers taken there, and
  each of these could move that decision:

    * The worst quantum on a slow machine: the busiest pieces (`orrery',
      `tide') plus a chord of note-ons, at 256 and at 128.
      `wasm/web/bench.mjs' is the harness; what is missing is the machine.
    * Chrome's and Firefox's main-thread-to-worklet delay on each OS, with
      a real output device rather than an offline context.
    * Background-tab behavior through a silent stretch of a piece, in both
      browsers.
    * Whether a dedicated worker's timers really are unthrottled in the
      browsers the room targets. Secondary sources only, so far.
